Subject: [PATCH] test(backend): force the admission race, because my own
 reverse mutation proved the old window too forgiving (ORD-0224 step 6
 follow-up)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Declared at 03:0x that mutant Y3 (the loser stops reporting the winner) is g3's product reverse
mutation. Re-running the identical campaign at `b2c6153` - a tree that differs from `f92f9e9` only by
a comment - reddened **3 of the 4** declared doors instead of 4: `test_g3_concurrent_open_generation_…`
survived, because with a favourable interleaving the later callers are answered from an
already-committed row and never reach the durable conflict at all.

A mutation that only sometimes reddens a door is not that door's reverse mutation, so the door is now
forced: `test_g3e_admission_race_five_callers_resolve_before_any_commit` parks every caller inside
`resolve_destination` until all five have finished resolving, so no row can be committed while the
others are still looking - the interleaving in which only the partial unique index plus the loser's
read-back can deliver "one operation, five callers".

The door does not claim to observe which caller collided (the product exposes nothing of the sort, and
inventing a counter for a test would be a product change); its strength is measured by the campaign,
which is run N times rather than once.

Readings at this commit: 20 collected across gates + weak-fix doors + measurement package,
19 passed / 1 failed (`test_g5_an_ai_run_that_changed_must_say_which_it_was`, pre-existing, ORD-0218);
the five g3 doors green on three repeats.
